eces to finalize.\n\nI'm noticing that srd001-testutils and srd003-format don't show up in the requirement_states at all—either their requirements were completed and removed, or they haven't been registered yet. Since codestatus shows them as \"not started,\" they likely haven't been registered. But the constraint is clear: I can only propose tasks for items with \"ready\" status, so if they're not in requirement_states, I shouldn't propose them. The question is whether missing SRDs mean they're untracked or just haven't been fed into the system yet.\n\nLooking at what I can actually work with, there are plenty of SRDs with \"ready\" requirements across multiple releases. Release 01.1 has srd006-cat with all its requirements ready, Release 01.2 has srd007-sponge fully ready, Release 01.3 has srd009-du ready, and Release 02.0 starts with srd013-true.\n\nBut I'm hitting a problem—the constraint says I can't read files or run commands, yet I'm supposed to check weight budgets from requirements.yaml to stay within the 4-task limit. Since I can't access the file, I'll need to assume each requirement has a default weight of 1, or the weights must be somehow derivable from what I already have.\n\nGiven that constraint, with a max weight of 4 per task, I can fit at most 4 requirements per task, which aligns with the 4 SRD sub-requirement limit anyway.\n\nNow I'm analyzing priorities by looking at the earliest incomplete releases. Release 00.0 is first, but most of its use cases are already implemented or not tracked. The only actionable items I can identify are srd011-magefiles R5.1 for uc001-magefiles and srd059-version R1.5, both related to cmd/version—and since the recent commit already built out the initial version binary, these are the next logical steps to complete.\n\nWithout access to the actual SRD contents, I need to work with what I have and focus on the release order. Looking at the ready requirements across releases, Release 00.0 has just 2 ready items, while Release 01.1 has srd006-cat with 13 ready requirements, Release 01.2 has srd007-sponge with 22, and Release 01.3 has srd009-du with 19—so there's a significant jump in available work once I move past the initial release.\n\nBut I'm noticing something odd: testutils and format aren't showing up in the requirement states at all, even though the code status marks them as implemented. That suggests the tracking might be out of sync with what's actually been completed in the codebase. Let me focus on Release 00.0 first since it's earliest, then move to 01.1 where there's much more ready work to pull from. Requirements not in requirement_states can't be proposed as tasks. Looking at the SRDs, srd011-magefiles has only R5.1 ready and srd059-version has R1.5 ready—that's one task for release 00.0. For release 01.1, srd006-cat alone has 13 ready requirements across two groups, which would need 4 tasks, but I'm constrained to 4 total tasks across all releases. the 00.0 requirements. Looking at the srd006-cat requirement IDs more carefully, I see R4.1 through R4.9 and R5.1 through R5.4—that's 13 requirements total. Since the numbering starts at R4, requirements R1-R3 must have already been completed and aren't showing up in the requirement_states.\n\nFor the task structure, I need to work within the constraints: maximum 4 tasks total, 4 SRD sub-requirements per task, weight capped at 4 per task, and implementation/tests split into separate tasks. For cmd/version, which already exists, I can handle both srd011 R5.1 and srd059 R1.5 in a single modify task with weight 2. For cmd/cat, which doesn't exist yet, I'm questioning whether a contract task is actually necessary for a main package with no exported API surface. The constraint is clear—I need a contract task before implementation tasks for any new package. For cmd/cat, that means defining the main function structure first, even though it's minimal. Looking at my task slots, I could do cmd/version's remaining requirements, then the cmd/cat contract, followed by splitting the implementation across two tasks to cover all eight requirements efficiently. I'm going in circles on this constraint interpretation. The rule requires a contract task before implementation tasks for new packages, but cmd/ packages don't export anything meaningful—just a main function. I think the most reasonable approach is to create a minimal contract task for cmd/cat that covers the main function signature, even though it feels like busywork, since the constraint is explicit about needing contracts for packages that don't already exist in the codebase.\n\nLooking at the actual requirements, I can map srd006-cat to rel01.1-uc001-cat, srd007-sponge to rel01.2-uc001-sponge, and srd009-du to rel01.3-uc001-du. The problem is that pkg/testutils and pkg/format are dependencies needed by these cmd/ packages, but they're not listed in the requirements, so I can't directly propose tasks for them—yet the cmd/ packages can't be tested without them.\n\nThis creates a dependency issue: testutils is marked as \"not started\" in code_status but has no registered requirements, which suggests the orchestrator hasn't registered them yet. Since I can only propose tasks for requirements that exist in requirement_states, I'll need to wait for those to be registered before I can propose testutils or format tasks.\n\nLooking at what's actually available across the releases, I can see that cat's implementation doesn't technically depend on testutils—only the tests do. Following the principle of splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, I could propose the cat implementation work first, with the test task deferred until testutils is ready.\n\nLet me map out what I can actually propose right now.\n\nFor the version binary tasks, I'm running into a problem: I don't know what R5.1 and R1.5 specifically require without reading the SRD files, and I can't access those. Without that detail, I can't write a meaningful task description