ous run\n2. Those groups don't exist in the SRD\n3. Those groups haven't been registered yet\n\nThe fact that only R4 and R5 groups are listed suggests the SRD has requirement groups R1-R5, and R1-R3 were completed. This aligns with the idea that some initial cat implementation exists but is incomplete.\n\nBut the code_status says cat's codestatus is \"not started\" and testfiles: 0. Hmm. Maybe R1-R3 are documentation/spec requirements (not code), and R4-R5 are the code requirements?\n\nActually, in this project's SRD structure, requirement groups within an SRD represent different functional areas, not phases. For cat, R4 and R5 might represent specific flag groups or features. R1-R3 might have been completed by a previous generation task.\n\nLooking at the commit history, I can see the pattern now: completed requirements get removed from the states file after they're done. For srd011, requirements R1.1-R4.1 were finished and removed, leaving only R5.1 as ready. So for srd006-cat, if R1-R3 groups aren't showing up, they might not exist in that SRD at all—the numbering could simply start at R4.\n\nI should just work with what's in requirement_states and trust those are the correct ready requirements. Now for the task itself: the stitch agent needs to read the SRD, check existing code, create or modify files, and meet acceptance criteria. For cmd/cat specifically, that means reading the SRD, checking pkg/sys for SIGPIPE handling, and creating the main implementation. But P2 says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, so I need to figure out whether tests should be included here or handled separately.\n\nLooking at the requirement numbering across other SRDs like srd013-true and srd012-yes, I can see patterns of how requirements are grouped, but without reading the actual SRD for cat, I can't tell which requirements are implementation-focused versus test-focused. Given the 4-task limit, I'll prioritize implementation tasks and defer test tasks to a future batch.\n\nLet me lock in my 4 tasks: the version command combining requirements from srd011-magefiles and srd059-version, then the cat core functionality pulling from srd006-cat's R4.1 through R4.4 to create the main implementation file.\n\nNext I'm breaking down the cat command flags into two separate tasks—one for R4.5 through R4.8 and another for the remaining flags plus tests covering R4.9 and R5.1 through R5.3. Each task builds on the previous one, with the flags task depending on the core implementation and the final task depending on the flags work.\n\nI realize the stitch agent will need the actual SRD documents to understand what each requirement group covers, so I should make sure the required_reading points directly to those specifications.\n\nOn the structure itself, I'm reconsidering whether a contract task is even necessary for cmd/cat. The constraint about needing contracts doesn't really apply here since a main package has no exported types or interfaces to stub out—the contract would be empty. Since the package doesn't exist yet, I can skip the contract and move straight to implementation tasks.\n\nNow I'm checking the release priorities. Release 00.0 has 2 ready requirements, but 01.1 with cat has 13 ready requirements, which is significantly more. Looking at the other releases—01.2 has 22, 01.3 has 19, and 02.0 has 62 spread across simpler utilities—I should tackle 00.0 first to clear those 2 requirements, then move to 01.1 for cat.\n\nOn the task ordering within releases, I'm weighing whether cat and version should be independent or if version should come first as foundational infrastructure. While they're separate packages, version is in an earlier release, so release priority takes precedence over alphabetical ordering. I'll keep them independent and let the orchestrator handle sequencing based on the release constraints.\n\nFor the cat tasks themselves, they need to be sequential since each one builds on the previous modifications to the same file. So task 1 feeds into task 2, which feeds into task 3.\n\nNow I'm moving on to writing out the YAML task descriptions that the stitch agent will use. Each task needs to be self-contained, so I'll need to specify the required reading materials—the SRD files themselves, any relevant architecture documentation, existing code files that need modification, and the SIGPIPE handler in pkg/sys.\n\nThe SRD files follow a consistent naming pattern in docs/specs/software-requirements/, so I'm mapping out which files correspond to which requirements. For the cat implementation, I'm working with cmd/cat/main.go which gets created in the first task and then modified in subsequent ones. I'm also realizing that tests need to be split into their own separate tasks per the constraint, so the cat tests would come later once testutils is available—they won't be part of these implementation tasks.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the task descriptions in YAML format, making sure each one is self-contained, references the correct SRD requirement IDs, and points to the right files. The version task will modify existing files since cmd/version already exists, while the cat tasks will create and then progressively enhance the main.go file. I'm realizing that R5.1 for the magefiles SRD likely involves testing the cmd/version binary, while srd059-version R1.5 probably addresses a specific aspect of the version output format or functionality. Rather than guessing at the exact requirements, I should have the stitch agent consult both SRDs directly and implement whatever remains after the recent commit that covered R1.1 through R4.1. I'll make sure the task title anchors to these specific requirement IDs as the constraint requires.\n\nI also need to ensure my groupings are deterministic—R4.1-R4.4 in one task, R4.5-R4.8 in the next, R4.9 plus R5.1-R5.3 in the third—so the same input always produces the same output. Since I can't read requirements.yaml, I'm assuming each requirement has the default weight of 1, which means my four-requirement tasks hit the weight limit exactly. If some requirements actually have higher weights, that could push me over, but without access to the file I'll proceed with this assumption.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the dependencies for the cat command implementation. It needs pkg/sys for the SIGPIPE handler, which is already implemented, so that's available. But cat doesn't need pkg/format since it doesn't do column alignment or human-readable formatting. For testing, it would use pkg/testutils, but that's not a runtime dependency.\n\nI should also note that I can't read the SRDs directly, so I'll base the design decisions on the architecture and shared protocols, and flag for the stitch agent to verify against the actual SRD for any concrete design choices. The architecture already specifies key requirements like SIGPIPE handling for all cmd utilities and differential testing using pkg/testutils, plus the reference binary from Homebrew.\n\nNow I'm breaking down the requirements across tasks: version needs 2 requirements from two SRDs, while cat spans 13 requirements that I'm distributing across multiple tasks to keep weights balanced at around 4 requirements each.\n\nFor the file structure, I'll be modifying the existing version command and creating the cat command in the first task, then refining it through subsequent tasks. The SRD files are already defined in the docs directory, so I just need to reference them correctly.\n\nI should verify whether version already has a test file from the previous commit—if it does, I'll modify both main.go and the test file; if not, I'll create them together in this task.\n\nNow I'm drafting the YAML descriptions for each task, starting with the version command task. I need to structure the deliverable with the required reading, file paths, and specific requirements tied to the SRD specifications. I'm realizing the requirements are too vague without actually reading the SRDs themselves. The stitch agent will need concrete instructions, but since I can't access those documents directly, I should rely on the required_reading section to guide them there. I'll frame the requirements around what the SRDs specify rather than guessing at the details, trusting that the agent will read those files as part of the task context. work. Let me finalize my output.\n\nFor the cat tasks, I know less about what R4.1-R5.4 specifically cover, but the stitch agent will read the SRD files. I'll frame the requirements as \"implement srd006-cat R4.1\" and so on, with required_reading pointing to the SRD. There's a key detail though: task 2 needs to know that task 1 already implemented R4.1-R4.4, so I should include cmd/cat/main.go in the required_reading as existing code to modify.\n\nNow I'll write the final output.\n\nLooking at the recent commit format, I'll use \"[stitch] component (srdNNN-name Rx.x, Ry.y, ...)\" for the task titles.\n\nTask 0: \"[stitch] cmd/version (srd011-magefiles R5.1, srd059-version R1.5)\"\nTask 1: \"[stitch] cmd/cat core (srd006-cat R4.1, R4.2, R4.3, R4.4)\"\nTask 2: \"[stitch] cmd/cat flags (srd006-cat R4.5, R4.6, R4.7, R4.8)\"\nTask 3: \"[stitch] cmd/cat output (srd006-cat R4.9, R5.1, R5.2, R5.3)\"\n\nChecking the weights: Task 0 has R5.1 (w=1) plus R1.5 (w=1) totaling 2.\n\nAll tasks are within the weight limit of 4, so I'm ready to generate the YAML output.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the structure for cmd/cat—since it's a main package without types or interfaces, I just need implementation and tests as separate tasks. The SRD requirements might bundle both together, but I'll let the stitch agent handle that based on what's actually specified. The key constraint is that a single task can't both implement and test the same feature, so I need to keep those clearly separated. I need to make sure I'm referencing the full file paths and including the right resources for the stitch agent. For the cat tasks, I should point to the SIGPIPE handler implementation, the SRD spec, and the existing main.go from previous work. I'm also confirming that cat doesn't depend on the format package since it's just a simple stream utility without any special formatting needs.\n\nNow I'm weighing whether to focus all my effort on completing cat across multiple tasks, or to mix in some simpler utilities like true and false that could be finished faster. Since the priority is the earliest incomplete release and cat is part of 01.1, it takes precedence—but I'm realizing that cat alone will consume a lot of task slots with its 13 requirements, leaving other utilities incomplete for longer. → 02.0 → ...\n\nP1 says I should focus on the earliest incomplete release. 00.0 is partially done with some use cases implemented and others still pending, while 01.1 hasn't been started yet. So after finishing the remaining 2 requirements for 00.0, I'll move on to 01.1 for the cat command.\n\nLet me think through the dependency chain for 01.1 though. It depends on 00.0 being complete, and 00.0 needs pkg/testutils and pkg/format to be finished. These aren't showing up in the requirement states, which makes me wonder if they're already done or just not registered yet. If testutils isn't available, I won't be able to write the tests for cat.\n\nBut here's the thing—the cat implementation itself can still be written since it only depends on pkg/sys, which is available. And looking at the architecture, cat doesn't actually need format (only ls and du do), so that's not a blocker either.\n\nNow I need to think about how to structure the actual tasks. The cat work breaks down into three tasks: creating the main file, then modifying it twice. Each task depends on the previous one, and for each one the stitch agent will need the SRD, the current state of the file, and the project rules.\n\nThe requirements are grouped sequentially by weight—R4.1-R4.4, then R4.5-R4.8, then R4.9 plus R5.1-R5.3. I don't know exactly what each requirement covers, but the stitch agent will read the SRD and understand the grouping. This approach is deterministic and respects the constraints.\n\nLet me finalize the output now. Actually, wait—I should verify what's in srd059-version since it only lists R1.5.\n\nLooking at the requirement states, srd059-version has just R1.5 marked ready, while srd011-magefiles has R5.1. The architecture notes that srd011-magefiles handles build automation and cmd/version is its only code generation target. These two single requirements—R1.5 from srd059-version and R5.1 from srd011-magefiles—form a natural pairing for one task.\n\nI'm deciding not to group them with a cat task since they belong to different releases and packages. Now I'm formatting the YAML output with the task definition, making sure the description field uses proper literal block scalar formatting with the nested YAML document inside.\n\nFormatting the YAML structure... I'm seeing that the acceptance criteria reference the SRD documents, which should provide the concrete details needed for verification. The stitch agent will have access to those documents, so delegating to them should work. But I'm also thinking about whether I need to make these more explicitly checkable—the planning constitution emphasizes verifiable outcomes without ambiguity. Let me consider if the current criteria are specific enough or if I should expand them with concrete examples of what passing would look like. For the cat implementation, I'll keep it focused on a single main.go file since the logic is straightforward. The design decisions are pretty standard—installing the SIGPIPE handler at startup, building with go build, treating \"-\" as stdin per GNU conventions, and routing errors to stderr while data goes to stdout. Now I'm ready to write out the complete response with the project summary, priority reasoning, and the full YAML task list, making sure each description is self-contained so the stitch agent can understand the tasks independently. Releas